5.7. Parsing an IPv4 Header with Bit-fields and a Union
Question: The first 32 bits of an IPv4 header contain several fields: Version (4 bits), IHL (4 bits), DSCP (6 bits), ECN (2 bits), and Total Length (16 bits). Write a program to parse a 32-bit integer into these fields using a union and a struct with bit-fields.


Click to see the solution

#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;

// A struct with bit-fields matching the IPv4 header layout
// Note: Bit-field order can be compiler-dependent (MSVC vs GCC)
// This example assumes a little-endian system where fields are laid out from LSB to MSB in memory
struct IPv4HeaderFields {
    uint32_t ihl     : 4;
    uint32_t version : 4;
    uint32_t ecn     : 2;
    uint32_t dscp    : 6;
    uint32_t total_length : 16;
};

// Union to interpret a 32-bit integer as a set of header fields
union IPv4Header {
    uint32_t raw_header;
    struct IPv4HeaderFields fields;
};

int main() {
    union IPv4Header packet;
    // Example 32-bit header value (in network byte order, but we'll parse it as a host integer)
    // 0x4500003c is a common example for a simple IPv4 packet
    packet.raw_header = 0x3c000045; // Stored in little-endian for this example to work

    printf("Parsing raw header: 0x%08X\n", packet.raw_header);
    printf("Version: %u\n", packet.fields.version);
    printf("IHL (Header Length): %u (words)\n", packet.fields.ihl);
    printf("DSCP: %u\n", packet.fields.dscp);
    printf("ECN: %u\n", packet.fields.ecn);
    printf("Total Length: %u (bytes)\n", packet.fields.total_length);

    return 0;
}
Answer: This program effectively uses a union to overlay a bit-field struct onto a 32-bit integer. This allows for efficient parsing of the integer into its distinct protocol fields, a common technique in network programming and embedded systems.

